(a) is_port_valid(int p)  ==  (p >= 0 && p <= 65535)
    Verified contiguously for every p in 0..200 and 65300..65600 plus samples (1000, 5000, 32767, 32768, 40000, 50000, 60000) and every negative in -60..-1 plus -80, -100, -65535, -65536, -2147483647/-8. No exceptions, no holes. 0 IS valid; 65535 valid; 65536 invalid; every negative invalid.
    Rust: fn is_port_valid(p: i32) -> bool { (0..=65535).contains(&p) }

(b) How the int is produced.

Step 1 - locate the port substring (only inside an authority):
  - An authority exists only if the raw URL contains "://". Everything before it is the scheme (may be empty: "://h:80" still yields port 80). If "://" is absent, NO authority is parsed and port stays at the sentinel ("h:80" -> port invalid; ":80" -> port invalid; "http:h:80" -> port invalid).
  - authority = text after "://" up to (but excluding) the FIRST occurrence of '/', '?' or '#', whichever comes first; or end of string.
      "http://h:80/a?b:99999" -> authority "h:80";  "http://h:8?0" -> authority "h:8" -> port 8 valid;  "http://h:8#0" -> port 8 valid.
  - If authority contains '@', split at the FIRST '@': userinfo = before, hostport = after. (First, not last: "http://a@h:80@b/x" -> port 80 VALID, which requires hostport="h:80@b"; "http://h:80@b/x" -> port INVALID because userinfo="h:80", hostport="b".)
  - In hostport, find the FIRST ':' (std::string::find, NOT rfind). If none -> port stays sentinel. If found -> host = hostport[..i], port_str = hostport[i+1..] (the WHOLE remainder, including any further colons).
      "http://h:80:99999/x" -> port_str "80:99999" -> 80 -> VALID (proves first-colon + trailing-junk tolerance).
      "http://h:99999:80/x" -> port_str "99999:80" -> 99999 -> INVALID.
      "http://[::1]:8080/x" -> first ':' is at index 1, port_str ":1]:8080" -> no digits -> throw -> sentinel -> INVALID. (This fully explains the "IPv6 port invalid" fact; "http://[1]:80/x" -> port_str "80" -> VALID.)
  - No url_decode is applied to the port substring: "http://h:%38%30/x" -> INVALID ("%38%30" is not "80").
  - No trim() is applied to the whole URL before parsing ("   http://h:80/x" leaves scheme "   http" invalid yet port 80 valid), and no trim is needed on the port substring because the conversion itself skips leading whitespace.

Step 2 - convert: it is exactly std::stoi(port_str) (i.e. strtol base 10 + range check) wrapped in try/catch:
    port = try { std::stoi(port_str) } catch (invalid_argument | out_of_range) { SENTINEL }
  Grammar accepted: [ \t\n\v\f\r]* [+|-]? [0-9]+ , then ALL trailing characters ignored.
    * Leading-byte sweep over all 256 byte values ("http://h:<b>80/x"): the ONLY bytes that keep the port parseable are 0x09,0x0A,0x0B,0x0C,0x0D,0x20 (the C-locale isspace set), '+' (0x2B) and '0'-'9'. Every other byte 1..255 (including '-' which yields a negative, '.', '_', 'x', all high bytes 128-255, Unicode/fullwidth/Arabic-Indic digits) gives line4 = 0.
    * Multiple/mixed leading whitespace then sign is fine: ": \t\n\v\f\r+65535" -> 65535 VALID. Whitespace BETWEEN sign and digits is not: ":+ 1", ":- 1", ": -1" -> INVALID (": -1" parses as -1).
    * Trailing-byte sweep over all 256 values ("http://h:80<b>/x"): every byte yields port 80 VALID except 0x40 '@' (which re-splits userinfo). So trailing junk is unconditionally ignored: "80abc", "65535x", "0x50"->0, "1e3"->1, "8.0"->8, "6 5"->6, "6553.5"->6553, "1_000"->1, "80junk:99"->80, "80::"->80.
    * Not atoi/strtol-cast-to-int: ":abc" -> INVALID (atoi would give 0 -> valid), and ":4294967376" (2^32+80) -> INVALID (an int truncation would give 80 -> valid). Also ":4294967296", ":8589934672", ":18446744073709551696", ":9223372036854775807/8" all INVALID.
    * Not a hand-rolled all-digits loop: ":+80", ": 80", ":80abc" are all VALID.
    * Leading zeros are handled by strtol semantics, arbitrarily many: ":0000...080" (29 chars) -> 80 VALID; ":0000...065536" -> INVALID (value, not length, decides).
    * The exception is caught locally: after a bad port the rest of the URL is still fully parsed ("http://h:abc/x" still gives host valid and path segment [x], exit 0, no terminate).

Step 3 - the sentinel: absent/unparseable port must map to an int OUTSIDE [0,65535]. It cannot be 0 (port 0 is valid while no-port is invalid). Its exact value is NOT observable through this driver (only is_port_valid's boolean is exposed and every out-of-range value collapses to false); -1 is the natural choice and is consistent with everything observed. Note that out_of_range (>INT_MAX) and "value simply > 65535" are behaviourally indistinguishable here - both print 0 on line 4 - so an implementation may equivalently do: parse to i64, and report valid iff the grammar matched AND 0 <= value <= 65535.

Equivalent Rust reference implementation of (b):
  fn parse_port(port_str: &str) -> i32 {          // returns -1 sentinel
      let b = port_str.as_bytes(); let mut i = 0;
      while i < b.len() && matches!(b[i], b' '|b'\t'|b'\n'|0x0B|0x0C|b'\r') { i += 1; }
      let neg = match b.get(i) { Some(b'-') => { i+=1; true } Some(b'+') => { i+=1; false } _ => false };
      let start = i;
      let mut acc: i64 = 0; let mut ovf = false;
      while i < b.len() && b[i].is_ascii_digit() {
          if !ovf { acc = acc*10 + (b[i]-b'0') as i64; if acc > 4_294_967_296i64*4 { ovf = true; } }
          i += 1;
      }
      if i == start { return -1; }                 // invalid_argument
      let v = if neg { -acc } else { acc };
      if ovf || v > i32::MAX as i64 || v < i32::MIN as i64 { return -1; } // out_of_range
      v as i32
  }

